# /etc/s5-poweroff-fix.conf — manual override for the auto-detection.
#
# YOU DO NOT NEED THIS in the normal case: s5-descubre-dgpu finds the discrete
# GPU, its audio function and its PCIe bridge by reading sysfs. Check that it
# gets it right first:
#
#     s5-descubre-dgpu
#
# You only need this file if that does NOT give the right answer. Whatever you
# set here wins; whatever you leave commented out is still auto-detected.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH DEVICES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Vendor of the discrete GPU. Defaults to NVIDIA. The integrated GPU is never
# touched: this filter is exactly what separates the two when both are present.
#   0x10de NVIDIA   0x1002 AMD/ATI   0x8086 Intel
#S5_VENDOR=0x10de

# Exact PCI addresses (`lspci -D` lists them). Use these if you have more than
# one discrete GPU, if it is an eGPU, or if detection picks the wrong device.
#
# CAREFUL: whatever you put here is what the module will shield. A wrong address
# points at some other device — your NVMe, your network card — and that must not
# be shielded. Verify with `lspci -D` first.
#GPU=0000:01:00.0

# HDMI audio function of the discrete GPU. By default it is derived (same
# address, function .1) and only accepted if it really is an audio-class device.
#AUDIO=0000:01:00.1

# To leave a part alone ON PURPOSE, set it to `none`. An EMPTY value does not
# work and never did: the detector cannot tell "the config set this to empty"
# from "nobody set it", so it just filled it back in and the audio function was
# shielded anyway — the opposite of what this file used to promise.
#AUDIO=none

# The PCIe bridge it hangs off. By default derived from the sysfs topology.
# It only gets the runtime-PM disable, which is per-device and harmless; its
# driver (pcieport) is NEVER touched, because it governs every port.
# `BRIDGE=none` also works, same as above.
#BRIDGE=0000:00:01.1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE REST — you almost certainly do not need any of this
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Battery to read for the energy witnesses. By default the first
# /sys/class/power_supply/BAT* is used, which is right on most laptops. Set it
# if yours is named differently or if you have two batteries and want a
# specific one. The value is a full path, not just a name.
# It reaches every reader, and getting there took two goes. Until 2026-08-14
# `s5-energy-log` — the one that writes the evidence log — ignored it, so on a
# two-battery laptop the hook and the meter read DIFFERENT cells and the Wh
# arithmetic between shutdown and boot meant nothing. Two of the `--tools`
# instruments were still missing it on 2026-08-15: `s5-queue` never read this
# file at all, and `s5-state-dump` read it six lines too late to matter. Neither
# fed the evidence arithmetic, but "every reader" has to be literally true or it
# is not worth writing down.
#S5_BAT=/sys/class/power_supply/BAT1

# GRUB flavour. Auto-detected by s5-descubre-grub: `grub2-*` + /boot/grub2 on
# Fedora/RHEL/SUSE, `grub-*` + /boot/grub on Debian/Ubuntu/Arch. Each of the
# three is detected on its own — a mixed install (Fedora commands, Debian
# directory) is not second-guessed. Check what it found with:
#
#     sudo s5-descubre-grub
#
# Set these only if that gets it wrong. Whatever you set here wins and is not
# even looked for. If any of them ends up empty, the GRUB branch of the policy
# disables itself and the shutdown proceeds normally — you lose the saving in
# the rare case where the dGPU stays awake, nothing else.
#
# The directory must be the one holding `grubenv` (that is what makes GRUB read
# the `custom.cfg` written next to it), NOT /boot/efi or /etc/grub.d.
#S5_GRUB_DIR=/boot/grub
#S5_GRUB_REBOOT=grub-reboot
#S5_GRUB_EDITENV=grub-editenv

# How long the rare-case policy waits, at shutdown, for the dGPU to fall to
# D3cold on its own before diverting through GRUB. 90 s by default. Lower it and
# you divert more often than you need (each divert costs you a reboot and two
# POSTs); raise it and every shutdown where the GPU is genuinely stuck takes
# longer before it gives up.
#S5_POLITICA_MAXWAIT=90

# Third-party shutdown hooks you have already looked at and consider fine.
# `s5-mitigacion-check` lists every executable in /usr/lib/systemd/system-shutdown
# that is not one of ours, because systemd runs them all during shutdown and it
# is worth knowing what else is in there. Naming them here stops it mentioning
# them. It does NOT silence the real check: a stray copy of one of OUR hooks
# (a `.bak-`, an `.orig`) is still a FAILURE, because systemd would run it in
# parallel with the good one. Space-separated basenames.
#S5_HOOKS_EXTRA="fwupd.shutdown mdadm.shutdown"

# Where the bootloader entries live, for the `--tools` measurement harness.
# Defaults to /boot/loader/entries, the BLS layout used by Fedora, RHEL and SUSE.
#
# Only two things read it, and neither is part of the fix: `s5-test -a`, which
# patches kernel arguments into every entry (keeping a backup) so a measurement
# can boot with them, and `uninstall.sh`, which restores that backup if you
# uninstall while a test is still armed — without which your entries would stay
# patched forever. If your distro puts them elsewhere, or you are not on BLS at
# all, `s5-test -a` fails loudly rather than patching the wrong directory.
#S5_BLS_DIR=/boot/loader/entries

# Which entry powers the machine off when the rare case is diverted through the
# bootloader. `app` (the default) uses the EFI halt application installed here, so
# the entry and the application live on the ESP; `builtin` uses systemd-boot's own
# "Power Off The System" entry, which needs `auto-poweroff yes` in loader.conf
# (loader v255+) and nothing installed at all.
#
# Why you might prefer `builtin`: arming is then a single EFI variable write, and
# `bootctl set-oneshot` does not touch the ESP — which matters because the ESP is
# already unmounted by the time the shutdown policy runs (measured 2026-08-11:
# boot.mount dies 100 ms before shutdown.target). With `app`, arming works without
# the ESP too (measured 2026-10-07: `bootctl --esp-path=<nonexistent> set-oneshot`
# reaches the write and fails only on permissions), but the entry and the
# application cannot be re-checked at that moment, so that check stays at install
# time.
#S5_BOOT_RUTA=app

# Optional fallback `.ko` for insmod, in case the akmod's modprobe fails.
# EMPTY BY DEFAULT, and that is deliberate: the akmod is the only mechanism that
# survives a kernel update, and a hand-built .ko stops working the moment the
# kernel changes. If you still want a net underneath, point this at it — and
# remember to rebuild it after every kernel update, or it becomes dead weight.
#S5_KO_RESPALDO=/usr/local/src/s5-pmrt-arm/s5_pmrt_arm.ko
